{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.5,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["U1"],
      "pins": ["U1.18", "U1.19", "U1.20", "U1.21"],
      "nets": ["CS0", "SCK", "CS1", "CS2"],
      "obs": "On the Microcontroller sheet, the global labels are attached one pin low: CS0 sits on pin 18 (GPIO3/ADC1_CH3), SCK on pin 19 (GPIO4/ADC1_CH4), CS1 on pin 20 (GPIO5/ADC2_CH0), and CS2 on pin 21 (GPIO6), while SO is correctly on pin 16 (GPIO10). GPIO7 (pin 22) carries no label.",
      "expected": "The specification states the SPI/CS lines are 'CS pins GPIO4/6/7' with 'SCK on GPIO5' and 'CS0/CS1/CS2, driven by ESP32 GPIO4/GPIO6/GPIO7 respectively', matching firmware constants (SPI2 on GPIO5, CS on GPIO4/6/7). The labels should therefore hang on GPIO4 (CS0), GPIO5 (SCK), GPIO6 (CS1), GPIO7 (CS2).",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "mcu.kicad_sch: 'GPIO03/ADC1_CH3 18' row with label box CS0; 'GPIO04/ADC1_CH4 19' with SCK; 'GPIO05/ADC2_CH0 20' with CS1; 'GPIO06 21' with CS2; 'GPIO10 16' with SO"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "SPI2 on GPIO5/GPIO10, CS pins GPIO4/6/7 ... CS0/CS1/CS2, driven by ESP32 GPIO4/GPIO6/GPIO7 respectively"}
      ],
      "impact": "The whole thermocouple SPI bus is wired to the wrong MCU pins: SCK is on GPIO4 instead of GPIO5, the three chip-selects are on GPIO3/GPIO4/GPIO5 instead of GPIO4/GPIO6/GPIO7, and GPIO7 is left unused. Firmware (which drives GPIO4/5/6/7/10) would never clock or select the MAX31855s correctly, so no valid temperatures are read; additionally GPIO3 is a boot-strapping pin now loaded by a thermocouple CS line.",
      "confidence": 0.55
    },
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["J7", "J8"],
      "pins": ["J7.1", "J7.2", "J8.1", "J8.2"],
      "nets": ["+12V", "GND"],
      "obs": "On the Power sheet the +12V vertical rail is dotted onto both pin 1 and pin 2 of each fan header (J7 and J8); pin 4 of each is marked no-connect, and the only GND symbol in that column hangs at the bottom of the same +12V line. No fan header pin is tied to GND as a return.",
      "expected": "The specification places only 'the two fan-header 12 V pins (J7/J8 pin 2)' on the +12V net; by standard 4-pin fan practice pin 1 is the fan ground return, which must connect to GND, not to +12V.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "power.kicad_sch: '+12V' arrow feeding a vertical line with junction dots to J7 pins 1 and 2 and J8 pins 1 and 2; pin 4 of each header marked X; GND symbol at bottom of that line"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "the two fan-header 12 V pins (J7/J8 pin 2) [on the +12V net]"}
      ],
      "impact": "The fans receive +12V on both power pins with no ground return, so neither fan spins and the open-collector tach signals (FAN_0_TACH/FAN_1_TACH) can never toggle, defeating the fan-stall fault detection; if the +12V rail and the GND symbol on that line are truly joined, it is a direct supply-to-ground short.",
      "confidence": 0.4
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The board deviates from the intended design in the thermocouple SPI interface: on the MCU sheet the CS0/SCK/CS1/CS2 labels are attached to GPIO3–GPIO6 (pins 18–21) instead of the specified GPIO4/5/6/7, leaving the firmware unable to drive the MAX31855 bus correctly; the fan headers also appear to have +12V on both power pins with no ground return, which is a secondary concern."
}

